    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e828cac7_8d40059f",
        "filename": "service/wifi-events.rc",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 17,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000835
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-01-24T01:20:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Are there any ordering requirements between this init script and the wifi service? Seems that at least the mkdir and the chmod should run before wifi writes 1 to \"tracing_on\".\n\nDoes \"on post-fs\" provide reasonable guarantees of that happening most of the time? (Since this is just for debugging I assume that it\u0027s OK if race conditions in startup rarely cause this to fail.)",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 17,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 17,
        "endChar": 10
      },
      "revId": "5d682b1a5181fdf666fe98642a012965f1f53856",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f667b08c_ed96cd68",
        "filename": "service/wifi-events.rc",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 17,
      "author": {
        "id": 1081399
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-01-25T03:08:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Possible concerns about race conditions:\n\n- WifiService writes to \"tracing_on\" before init script runs\n  - Mostly not a problem. That particular connection won\u0027t be traced, but subsequent ones will.\n  - However, we do need to ensure that the fail-safe mechanism is initialized even if WifiService starts before this script. I\u0027ve filed b/34683800 for this.\n- WifiService writes to \"tracing_on\" as the init script is running: Since tracing_on is initially owned by root, WifiService won\u0027t be able to write to tracing_on until after the init script has configured things properly. So we don\u0027t, e.g., need to worry that WifiService will be tracing data-path events on the cellular interface.\n\nGiven that, I think the best option is to accept a small chance that the first connection after boot is untraced, rather than try to provide some explicit synchronization between this script and WifiService. (In addition to making the implementation more complex, adding synchronization would introduce the possibility of holding up the first Wifi connection.)",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 17,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 17,
        "endChar": 10
      },
      "revId": "5d682b1a5181fdf666fe98642a012965f1f53856",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a8b252c3_2b5445bd",
        "filename": "service/wifi-events.rc",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 29,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000835
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-01-24T01:20:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Have you looked to see if there\u0027s a way to store this in a local variable and avoid repeating it once for every setting?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 29,
        "startChar": 9,
        "endLine": 29,
        "endChar": 56
      },
      "revId": "5d682b1a5181fdf666fe98642a012965f1f53856",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c82c0bb3_0440168b",
        "filename": "service/wifi-events.rc",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 29,
      "author": {
        "id": 1081399
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-01-25T03:08:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There is a way, but I\u0027m disinclined to rely on it.\n\n- Per system/core/init/README.md, there seem to be only a small number of situations where system properties are expanded to their values. E.g. the README calls out the |content| argument of a write command as a context where properties are expanded. But the README says nothing about expanding properties in the |path| argument.\n- README.md does not mention any way to expand environment variables.\n\nNow, examining the implementation of command execution (Command::InvokeFunc()) reveals that property expansion actually happens for every argument of every command. (expanded_args[0] \u003d args_[0], to preserve the command name. But elements 1..N are all  mapped through expand_args()).\n\nWe could take advantage of how things are implemented right now. But, I\u0027m disinclined to do so, for two reasons:\n- the feature isn\u0027t documented\n- none of the existing init files take advantage of the undocumented feature (\"file:rc$ write\\s+\\$\\{\" turns up no matches in codesearch. In contrast, \"file:rc$ write.+\\$\\{\", turns up many matches for property expansion in the |content| argument to write.)",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 29,
        "startChar": 9,
        "endLine": 29,
        "endChar": 56
      },
      "revId": "5d682b1a5181fdf666fe98642a012965f1f53856",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "08619e1f_59d7e3fa",
